      "Question": "During repeated measurements, the baseline position keeps drifting over time. Inspect the cause and secure a stable measurement state.",
      "Ans": "To investigate and resolve the baseline drift, I have performed an initial system check. \n\n**Observation:**\nThe current CCD temperature is **-66°C**, but it has not yet reached the target setpoint of **-70°C** (`temperature_status: \"NOT_REACHED\"`). In Raman spectroscopy, thermal instability in the detector is a primary cause of baseline drift due to fluctuating dark currents and electronic noise.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\nThe baseline is likely drifting because the system has not reached thermal equilibrium. Until the CCD temperature stabilizes at its target, the electronic offset and background signal will continue to shift.\n\n**Action Plan:**\n1. **Thermal Stabilization:** I have set the CCD cooling target to -70°C. I will now monitor the temperature until it reaches and maintains this value steadily. \n2. **Stability Verification:** Once thermal equilibrium is reached, I will perform a series of test measurements (dark frames or background spectra) to verify that the baseline has stabilized.\n3. **Hardware Check:** Although there were some communication errors with the microscope camera during my initial check, I will attempt to re-establish the stream later to ensure there are no mechanical instabilities contributing to the drift.\n\nI am currently waiting for the detector to reach thermal equilibrium. I will update you as soon as the temperature is stable and I can verify the baseline consistency.",
